\textbf{Tips for Rotation}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Start with 4D rotations (Q/W, A/S, Z/X) to observe unique 4D effects, like the tesseract’s cell flips or 5-cell’s vertex cycles.
    \item Use slow speeds (0.25x) with nudges for precise analysis, especially when studying a single plane.
    \item Combine rotations with axis display (keys 1–6) to visualize the plane being rotated (e.g., Q with xw axes).

\subsection{Marking Controls}
Marking highlights vertices or faces to track cells during rotation. Select a tool before marking.

\subsubsection{Color and Tool Selection}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Color Palette}: Click one of 8 color swatches to select an active color (white border indicates selection).
    \item \textbf{Erase Button}: Select to remove individual vertex markings via point-and-click.
    \item \textbf{Clear All Button}: Removes all markings (vertices and faces) from the current object.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Cell-Level Marking}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Mark Cell Vertices}: Select a color, then toggle a cell button (e.g., W+ for tesseract, Cell 0 for 5-cell). Marks the cell’s vertices with colored dots.
    \item \textbf{Mark Cell Faces}: Select a color, then toggle a cell button. Highlights the cell’s faces with semi-transparent polygons. Tesseract faces show directional symbols; 5-cell faces are plain triangles.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Point-and-Click Marking}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Mark a Single Vertex}: Select a color, click a white vertex to add a colored dot (16 for tesseract, 5 for 5-cell).
    \item \textbf{Erase a Single Vertex}: Select Erase, click a marked vertex to remove its last color.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Face Marking System}
For the tesseract, each cell’s six faces have unique symbols to indicate orientation (not available for 5-cell’s triangular faces):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{X-Axis}: Horizontal arrow (right for +, left for -).
    \item \textbf{Y-Axis}: Vertical arrow (up for +, down for -).
    \item \textbf{Z-Axis}: Circle with diagonal line (out for +, in for -).
    \item \textbf{W-Axis}: Square with dot (out for +) or cross (in for -).
\end{itemize}
Symbols appear on marked faces, aiding in tracking 4D orientation.

\subsection{Mark Axis of Rotation for a Given Plane}
Toggle axis display to visualize rotation planes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Keys 1–6}: Toggle axes for planes:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item 1: X, W (xw-plane, Q/W)
            \item 2: Y, W (yw-plane, A/S)
            \item 3: Z, W (zw-plane, Z/X)
            \item 4: X, Y (xy-plane, E/R)
            \item 5: X, Z (xz-plane, D/F)
            \item 6: Y, Z (yz-plane, C/V)
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Key 0}: Toggles axis labels (“X”, “Y”, “Z”, “W”) at both axis endpoints.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Axis Appearance}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{W-axis}: Cyan
    \item \textbf{X-axis}: Magenta
    \item \textbf{Y-axis}: Yellow
    \item \textbf{Z-axis}: Green
    \item \textbf{Length}: Tesseract: ±1.5 (150 units, extends beyond faces); 5-cell: ±0.707 (~70.7 units, approximates face distance).
    \item \textbf{Labels}: Plane labels (e.g., “1: xw”) at one axis endpoint, offset to avoid overlap; axis labels (with key 0) at both endpoints.
\end{itemize}
Axes are thick (width=2.5) and drawn after face meshes for visibility.

\subsection{Mapping the Cell Labels}
Cell labels correspond to geometric definitions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Tesseract (Hypercube)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{X-}: Cube with negative X-coordinate vertices.
            \item \textbf{X+}: Cube with positive X-coordinate vertices.
            \item \textbf{Y-}: Cube with negative Y-coordinate vertices.
            \item \textbf{Y+}: Cube with positive Y-coordinate vertices.
            \item \textbf{Z-}: Cube with negative Z-coordinate vertices.
            \item \textbf{Z+}: Cube with positive Z-coordinate vertices.
            \item \textbf{W-}: Cube with negative W-coordinate vertices (initial inner cube).
            \item \textbf{W+}: Cube with positive W-coordinate vertices (initial outer cube).
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{5-Cell (Hyper Tetrahedron)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textbf{Cell 0–4}: Each cell is a tetrahedron formed by four of the five vertices, labeled internally as Cell 0 to Cell 4.
        \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

% Tips for Visualization section
\section{Tips for Visualization}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Start Simple}: Begin with the tesseract, using Method 1 to observe the inside-out flip. Then explore the 5-cell, focusing on vertex cycles.
    \item \textbf{Use Markings}: Mark W- and W+ (tesseract) or Cell 0 (5-cell) to track key cells during rotation.
    \item \textbf{Leverage Axes}: Toggle axes (1–6) and labels (0) to understand rotation planes, especially for 4D rotations (xw, yw, zw).
    \item \textbf{Experiment with Speed}: Use slow speeds (0.25x) for detailed analysis, faster speeds (1.50x) for dynamic observation.
    \item \textbf{Resize Window}: Maximize the window to compare tesseract and 5-cell sizes, noting their scaled projections.
    \item \textbf{Toggle Objects}: Switch between tesseract and 5-cell (`T`) to contrast their complexity (8 vs. 5 cells).
\end{itemize}

